---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import { GITHUB_URL, LINKEDIN_URL, OWNER_NAME, OWNER_LOCATION, AUTOMATION_BRIEF_URL } from '../data/site';
---

<BaseLayout title="AI Automation Consulting & Local LLM Deployment">
  <Hero />

  <div class="geo-divider"></div>

  <!-- Three benches -->
  <section class="section container">
    <p class="eyebrow">The workshop</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Three benches, one craft.</h2>
    <p class="section-subtitle">
      Automate a workflow, equip your engineering team, or bring a physical
      design to life. Explore the public projects behind each kind of work.
    </p>

    <div class="lanes-grid reveal-stagger">
      <a href="/animus" class="card card-copper lane">
        <svg class="lane-icon" viewBox="0 0 40 40" aria-hidden="true">
          <path d="M4 12 H36 M4 20 H36 M4 28 H36" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2" opacity="0.5"/>
          <circle cx="14" cy="12" r="2.4" fill="currentColor"/>
          <circle cx="26" cy="20" r="2.4" fill="currentColor"/>
          <circle cx="18" cy="28" r="2.4" fill="currentColor"/>
        </svg>
        <p class="lane-tag">Agents &amp; automation</p>
        <h3>Animus &amp; custom harnesses</h3>
        <p class="lane-motto">Intelligence that stays home.</p>
        <p class="lane-desc">
          The open-source Animus Ferric harness — Rust, built for 1B–14B local
          models — plus paid implementations: llama.cpp deployments, on-prem
          agents, business process automation.
        </p>
        <span class="lane-proof">Animus Ferric · diploid · edge deployments</span>
        <span class="lane-link">Visit the bench &rarr;</span>
      </a>

      <a href="/skills" class="card card-thread lane">
        <svg class="lane-icon" viewBox="0 0 40 40" aria-hidden="true">
          <circle cx="15" cy="20" r="9" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/>
          <circle cx="25" cy="20" r="9" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" opacity="0.65"/>
        </svg>
        <p class="lane-tag">Skills &amp; training</p>
        <h3>Agent skills &amp; workshops</h3>
        <p class="lane-motto">Skills that outlive subscriptions.</p>
        <p class="lane-desc">
          Portable agent workflows — Sprint Loops, GECK, MDR — plus custom
          skills and hands-on team workshops. Adapted to the tools your team
          uses, including Claude Code, Codex, and local harnesses.
        </p>
        <span class="lane-proof">Sprint Loops · GECK · MDR</span>
        <span class="lane-link">Visit the bench &rarr;</span>
      </a>

      <a href="/design" class="card card-madder lane">
        <svg class="lane-icon" viewBox="0 0 40 40" aria-hidden="true">
          <path d="M20 6 L34 14 V26 L20 34 L6 26 V14 Z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/>
          <path d="M20 6 V20 M20 20 L34 14 M20 20 L6 14 M20 20 V34" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" opacity="0.6"/>
        </svg>
        <p class="lane-tag">Design &amp; fabrication</p>
        <h3>CAD &amp; 3D printing</h3>
        <p class="lane-motto">Drawn, printed, held.</p>
        <p class="lane-desc">
          Commissioned designs — jigs &amp; fixtures, corporate gear, gaming
          assets, manufacturing equipment — and 1-on-1 Autodesk Fusion lessons
          in geometry and additive manufacturing.
        </p>
        <span class="lane-proof">Jetson Orin Nano Super Case · HexQuest</span>
        <span class="lane-link">Visit the bench &rarr;</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="geo-divider"></div>

  <section class="section container editorial-split">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">A practical first commission</p>
      <h2 class="section-title editorial-heading">Start with one workflow.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-stack">
      <p>
        A report assembled by hand. Files copied between systems. An internal
        task that needs judgment before the next step. Bring the recurring work
        you want to change, and we'll define what a useful first build must do.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Considering local AI?</strong> Start with a feasibility assessment:
        your task, available hardware, data boundary, and a clear way to measure
        whether the result is good enough. Model size alone cannot answer that.
      </p>
      <div class="action-row">
        <a href={AUTOMATION_BRIEF_URL} class="btn btn-primary">Scope an automation</a>
        <a href="/services/#automation-assessment" class="text-link">See the starting scope</a>
      </div>
      <p class="section-note">A short email is enough. Scope, price, and deliverables are agreed before the build.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="geo-divider"></div>

  <section id="about" class="section container editorial-split">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">The person at the bench</p>
      <h2 class="section-title editorial-heading">Built by {OWNER_NAME}.</h2>
      <p class="founder-location">Independent developer &amp; designer · {OWNER_LOCATION}</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-stack">
      <p>
        I'm Charles. Thread &amp; Signal brings my software and design work into
        one studio: Rust and Python development, local AI systems, agent workflows,
        and CAD for parts you can make. You work directly with the person building it.
      </p>
      <p>
        My public work connects those disciplines. <a href="/animus/" class="text-link">Animus Ferric</a>
        explores how small local models use tools. <a href="/skills/" class="text-link">Sprint Loops</a>
        gives agent-assisted development a documented process. The
        <a href="/design/" class="text-link">Jetson Orin Nano Super Case</a> takes the work from code to a printable enclosure.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Local deployment starts with a boundary you can explain.</strong>
        That means deciding where inference runs, which tools can reach the network,
        and what a person must approve. Those choices are part of the build.
      </p>
      <div class="action-row">
        <a href={GITHUB_URL} class="text-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Explore my GitHub</a>
        <a href={LINKEDIN_URL} class="text-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Professional background</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="geo-divider"></div>

  <section class="section container editorial-split">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">From the workshop journal</p>
      <h2 class="section-title editorial-heading">Before you buy more hardware.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-stack">
      <p>
        Can a local LLM run your business workflow? Work through the questions
        that matter: task fit, memory, data access, evaluation, and maintenance.
      </p>
      <a href="/blog/local-llm-business-workflow/" class="text-link">Read the local LLM feasibility guide &rarr;</a>
      <div class="action-row">
        <a href="/contact" class="btn btn-primary">Commission a build</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .lanes-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 280px), 1fr));
    gap: 1.5rem;
  }

  .lane {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .lane:hover {
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .lane-icon {
    width: 40px;
    height: 40px;
    color: var(--lane);
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }

  .lane-tag {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
  }

  .lane h3 {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
  }

  .lane-motto {
    font-family: var(--font-heading);
    font-style: italic;
    font-weight: 450;
    color: var(--lane);
    margin-bottom: 0.9rem;
  }

  .lane-desc {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: 0.93rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
    flex-grow: 1;
  }

  .lane-proof {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.73rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    margin-bottom: 0.85rem;
  }

  .lane-link {
    color: var(--lane);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .founder-location {
    font-size: var(--text-small);
    color: var(--text-muted);
    max-width: var(--measure-heading);
  }
</style>
